export const essays = {
  "guides/great-dane-health-issues.html": `<p>A tight belly and a dog who retches without bringing anything up is the picture I still move for. That emergency is bloat, gastric dilatation-volvulus. The deep chest gives the stomach room to fill with gas and twist. I have carried dogs to the car with the abdomen already drum-tight. Lingering on the kitchen floor lets that emergency tighten. The clinic gets the call while the car is moving so a surgery team can be ready.</p>
<p>Gastropexy belongs in the talk at the spay or the neuter, while the abdomen is already open for that operation. I raise it with the surgeon who will be in the belly, and the decision goes in the record. Dilated cardiomyopathy is the heart disease that empties a dog who still looks enormous. The walk gets shorter. A cough shows up after midnight. Sometimes he folds on the stairs. Those signs send us to a cardiologist for an ultrasound of the heart muscle. I want that image on the calendar near year 1, and I want a real listen again by year 2 if the first visit was only a stethoscope.</p>
<p>Hips fail in a way an owner can see before any film exists. Hip dysplasia loosens the ball and socket. The dog bunny-hops up from a sit, slides the hind feet wide, or balks at the car. Radiographs are the pictures I ask for. Wobbler syndrome lives in the neck. The Dane I took home scuffed his rear toenails until the quick showed, and his back end wandered when the hallway was slick. He guarded his neck when a collar came over his ears. Imaging of the cervical spine is what I request once that gait outlasts a single clumsy afternoon.</p>
<p>Osteosarcoma is the bone cancer that turns a limp into a firm, warm swelling on a long bone. Rest fails to retire the limp. The leg looks thicker than its pair. Films of that bone are the first look, and oncology takes the case from there. Color genetics sit in the same health talk. Pairing merle with merle can leave puppies deaf and can damage their eyes. Merle is the genetic base of harlequin, so a spotted pedigree gets that question too. Fawn, brindle, blue, black, harlequin, and mantle can all be correct coats. Paint tells me nothing about the stomach, the heart, or the hips.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual chapter on gastric dilatation-volvulus</a> is the reference I hand someone who has only met the word bloat on a forum. At home the job is narrower: notice the empty retch, notice the midnight cough, and keep the gastropexy decision tied to the surgery date already on the book.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-temperament.html": `<p>The lamp died because a tail at chest height cleared the end table on the way to the kitchen. Living beside a male whose withers cleared 30 inches rearranged the furniture more than the schedule. He wanted whichever room I was using, and he arrived in a few strides. Females I have handled near the 28 inch minimum still fill a hallway when they turn around. Plenty of grown dogs stand taller than those minimums, and the taller ones lean. The lean is affection with a shoulder behind it. I teach a stand-back, palm flat on the chest, before a guest reaches the rug.</p>
<p>What I have lived with is a long watch and a short burst. The driveway gets noticed. One deep bark rolls out, then the dog checks my face to learn whether the person belongs. Play is real, and it stays clumsy until it has edges. I hold a tug low so the neck stays level, and I stop the game when the feet start to skate. Toy 1 is a soft tug saved for evening. Toy 2 is a stuffed animal that gets put away, so the game has a start and a finish. A harsh shout shuts these dogs down faster than it teaches anything. The cue stays quiet. The pay is the toy, or the chance to follow me to the door.</p>
<p>Coat color never sorted character in my house. A gold coat, a striped coat, a dilute gray-blue, a solid black, a patched white, and a dark-blanket mantle all wanted the same patch of rug behind my heels. The short coat lets me feel a shiver when the floor is cold, and a sweater on a January night stops the pacing that comes with that shiver. Once the house goes quiet they settle hard. Mine slept with his spine along the couch and his nose aimed down the hall, still on duty inside the dream.</p>
<p>Strangers go better when practice happens while the dog is young and the human stays calm. We sit in the clinic lot. People pass. He gets to watch, which suits a body built for a long look, and then he gets to leave. Steering a giant into a hug from a stranger teaches him that people corner him. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/great-dane/">AKC Great Dane breed page</a> sketches the large, people-focused dog I have been feeding. I trust that outline, and I trust the palm-on-chest lesson the day a delivery driver steps inside.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Dust shows first on a black coat and last on a fawn one. The jacket is short, so the tools that earn their keep are a rubber curry and a hound glove. The steel comb stays in the drawer. I glove with the lie of the hair, from the poll down the neck, across the deep chest, and along the thigh. Pass number 1 midweek puts loose hairs into the glove. In spring I repeat the work on evening 2, because even a short coat lets go of winter skin. Brindle hides dirt until my palm comes away gray. Blue coats pick up a rusty film off wet grass. Harlequin and mantle dogs get a white-towel test. I wipe the white, I look at the cloth, and the cloth decides whether a bath can wait.</p>
<p>Soap stays occasional. Too much of it on this coat dries the skin over the ribs, where the fat layer is thin. A small dab, a rinse until the water runs clear, then a towel on the chest, the groin, and the pocket where the ear meets the skull. Nails on a dog this heavy matter every week I hear them click on the tile. Ears get a look and a dry cotton ball. A tick is easier to spot here than under a plush jacket, so my thumb parts the hair while the glove is still on my hand. Teeth get a finger brush along the canines and the big molars while he is already standing for the curry.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/great-dane">VCA Hospitals Great Dane care sheet</a> matches the plain coat I have been gloving. Plain still means hands on the dog. A shiny fawn shoulder can hide a scab until the curry finds it. I want that find at home, in daylight, before a collar turns it into a hot spot.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-exercise-guide.html": `<p>The old job was a hunt after large game and a watch around a house. The week I build from that history is steady walking plus a thinking task, with rest packed around meals. I have weighed a light adult female at 110 pounds and a tall finished male at 175. Both lived under the same stomach rule. Fetch, wrestling, and a long trot stay off the clock during the hour after the bowl. The deep chest is the reason. A drum-tight belly after work is an emergency pattern I refuse to meet on a trail. Walk, then rest, then eat, then rest again.</p>
<p>The years I keep in mind for this breed often run 7 to 10, and the joints haul a lot of dog through every one of them. Year 1 is grass walking, sniffing, and easy sits. By year 2 I lengthen one outing if the gait is clean and someone has already looked at the hips. Forced running on pavement stays off the plan while growth plates are open. A puppy food made for giant dogs, fed so the scale climbs slowly, is the bowl half of that same idea.</p>
<p>Monday and Thursday are neighborhood walks, long enough for a real sniff and finished before he is cooked. Tuesday is yard work: stays, a recall on a long line, and a light carry only after the dog is mature. Wednesday is a down day with a food puzzle on a mat. Friday repeats a walk on a fresh block. Saturday is a field where he trots at his own pace and quits when he wants. Sunday is off, a chew and a nap. Heat deletes the midday slot. Cold treats this body more kindly than July, though the short coat still wants a jacket of his own if we stand around in wind.</p>
<p>Rear toes that scuff, or shoulders that shorten their stride, cut the week in half until I know why. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/great-dane/">Great Dane history and build at AKC</a> is where I send people who ask what that tall frame was shaped to do. The answer rewrites the calendar: more walking and watching, less frantic ball, and a quiet stretch after dinner that counts as part of the work.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Cash stays in my account until the folder is on the table. I want hip results on both parents, a heart clearance aimed at dilated cardiomyopathy, and a plain answer on coat genetics. I ask whether either parent carries merle. Putting two merle dogs together can leave the babies deaf, and it can harm their eyes. Harlequin sits on that merle base, so the question covers a spotted litter as well. Fawn, brindle, blue, black, and mantle babies get the identical folder. Color is the easy column.</p>
<p>Growth has to stay slow. I feed a puppy kibble labeled for giant dogs, and I turn down any plan that chases maximum size by the half-year mark. Height will arrive. A male is aimed at a 30 inch minimum at the shoulder, and lots of grown males go taller. A female is aimed at a 28 inch minimum. The chest deepens later, which is why a gangly baby can look narrow and still be on the right road. Through year 1, outings stay walks and play on soft ground. The stomach-tack conversation waits until he is altered, often near year 2, when that operation is already on the book.</p>
<p>Feet, ears, and a fake standing exam happen every evening so the clinic scale is boring by the time he is tall. The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program for hip and cardiac clearances</a> is the stack I lay beside the breeder's papers. A kennel that cannot find those sheets is a kennel I leave, deposit still in the bank.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-shedding-guide.html": `<p>What gathers on the baseboard is short, stiff, and the color of the dog who left it. Fawn needles are tan. Black needles shout on a pale sheet. Brindle sheds a blend. Blue sheds a gray dust. Harlequin and mantle give up both white and dark, and the white hairs are the ones I pull out of a sweater. There is no woolly undercoat releasing in clumps. Follicles on the throat, over the brisket, along the ribcage, and down the rear thigh let go of a short hair, and that hair drifts because it weighs almost nothing.</p>
<p>The thickest scatter sits where a collar rubs and where a shoulder presses the couch. Friction pops the jacket loose. A glove catches it before it hits the floor when I keep a rhythm: a full pass on day 1 of my week, a quicker pass on day 2. Spring still raises the amount. Skin cycles even on a cropped-short jacket. Bath water loosens a lot at once. Glove, then bath, then glove again after he is dry, or the towel becomes the collection bag.</p>
<p>Black pants tell on me when I stand up. A damp rubber glove gathers whatever the curry missed along the hock. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/great-dane">VCA notes on living with a Great Dane</a> describe this same short jacket that turns up along the floorboards. The strand on the cushion was growing from the outer coat yesterday. It was never tucked away as a secret winter layer.</p>`,

  "guides/great-dane-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>I have put candles on a cake and I have also stood in a treatment hallway much earlier than any family planned. The years I quote often fall between 7 and 10. That clock is short, and the cuts have names. Bloat, the twist of gastric dilatation-volvulus, can finish a day. The belly swells. The dog heaves and nothing comes up. A dog who survives still risks a repeat if the stomach was left free to move. I want the tack discussed on the day he is altered.</p>
<p>Dilated cardiomyopathy cuts lives short by weakening the pump until a cough, a collapse, or a death with no rehearsal is what people meet. Osteosarcoma cuts them by taking a limb and then traveling. Wobbler syndrome steals the easy years when pressure in the neck makes walking unsafe. Hip dysplasia steals comfortable months when the joint hurts enough that a huge dog quits moving, and muscle falls away once the walks stop.</p>
<p>From age 1 to age 2 I want the heart heard with attention and the hips on film, so a finding becomes a plan. A scooped puppy diet and a waist my hands can locate are the daily levers I actually hold. The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck coverage of dilated cardiomyopathy in dogs</a> is what I open again when someone asks how a failing pump moves the calendar. Dogs I watched reach the far side of that 7 to 10 year stretch had a person who treated a midnight cough as a heart question.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-great-danes-get.html": `<p>The tape sits at the withers, dog standing square, head level. Male height starts at 30 inches and often finishes above that line. Many grown males are simply taller. Female height starts at 28 inches. I have stood a light adult bitch on the clinic scale and read 110 pounds, and I have read 175 on a tall male whose chest had finished dropping. Both numbers can belong to correct dogs, because the registry is measuring height. A short back stacked with extra pounds is a different shape from the one I am feeding toward. I want a deep chest, a long leg, and a waist a hand can find without a search.</p>
<p>Youngsters fool people in the parking lot. Early on, the feet look borrowed. By month 12 most of the leg length I am going to get is already there, and the body still looks narrow. The second project is fill. From month 18 toward month 24 the sternum drops, the thigh thickens, and the head finishes its planes. I have watched depth keep coming after height stalled. Some were still refining at age 2 even though the withers had quit climbing around age 1. That lag is why the bowl stays measured. Kibble meant for big puppies, scooped, beats a bag that brags about a huge dog by the next holiday.</p>
<p>Dogs in one litter can land on either side of those height lines. I ask for the parents' mature inches, taken with a wicket, before I listen to a guess. A dam whose shoulder sits at 28 inches and a sire well clear of 30 can throw a spread. Harlequin, mantle, fawn, brindle, blue, and black grow on one curve. The wicket ignores paint. Every month I write the date, the inches, and whether I can feel ribs. A spurt followed by a pause is ordinary. Extra calories aimed at a pound target worry me, because the bone is still soft while the body is getting heavy.</p>
<p>Repeated stairs can wait while that fill is underway. Grass is the surface I trust under those big feet. People meet an adolescent around month 12, see the ears up in the air, and announce that growth is finished. The sternum still has dropping left. The coupling still has to catch the leg. I use the same spot every time, thumb on the shoulder blade, tape straight. Nobody leans the dog forward to steal an extra inch for the photo.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/great-dane/">AKC height expectations for Great Danes</a> stays open on my phone while I explain why a pound chart is the wrong scoreboard. Withers height, males from 30 inches upward with many adults taller still, females from 28, deep chest arriving late: that is the size story I will defend. The 110 and the 175 are scale readings I have seen, useful for a harness label, useless as a substitute for the tape.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-health-issues.html": `<p>The honk in my hallway is usually a collapsing trachea. A Pomeranian coughs like a goose when the doorbell rings or when a collar yanks a tiny neck. The airway is a soft tube. I switch the walk gear to a harness, I keep excitement from stacking on itself, and I ask the clinic to listen. If the cough keeps its shape, I ask for imaging of the trachea. Show weight sits between 3 and 7 pounds. Extra fat on a neck that small makes every cough meaner.</p>
<p>Luxating patellas are the knee chapter. The dog skips, holds a hind leg up for several steps, then sets it down and trots on. I feel the kneecap while the leg is soft. A patella grade belongs in the file. Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is the other young-dog problem in that neighborhood of the body. One hind leg hurts, the thigh wastes, and films of the femoral head explain a limp that the kneecap exam already failed to solve. Knee hands and hip films answer different questions, and I want both named.</p>
<p>Dental disease is constant in a mouth this small. Tartar packs the molars. A smell returns a week after a chew. Gums redden along the line. A dental under anesthesia is the fix once the mouth is that far along. At home I use a finger brush on the outer surfaces. Weekday 3 and weekday 5 are the evenings I actually do it, because a plan tied to two named days survives a clinic schedule better than a vague promise.</p>
<p>Alopecia X shows up as hair leaving the trunk while the head and the legs stay fluffy. Skin can darken. The dog still eats and still alarms at the door. I treat the coat loss as a diagnosis only after the clinic has ruled out hormone problems that look similar, and I promise nothing about a regrowth date. Progressive retinal atrophy starts as hesitation when the room dims. Pupils look wide. An ophthalmology exam is the test, and I want it repeated across the years, because one clear puppy check is a snapshot.</p>
<p>Hypoglycemia belongs to tiny puppies. A late meal, a limp body, a glassy stare. Several small feedings spread through the day are how I keep the sugar steady in those first months, and a puppy who goes flat needs the clinic the same hour. The years I have watched often cover 12 to 16, long enough for teeth, airway, and eyes to each take a turn. The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC orthopedic records for toy dogs</a> is where I point people who want the patella and hip paperwork named in one place.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-temperament.html": `<p>The kitchen windowsill has a worn oval the size of a fox face. That oval is the post. These dogs are toy spitz, built alert, and the alertness comes out of the mouth. Doorbells, a truck, a leaf on the glass: the bark is the report. I can live with the voice when it has an off switch. I wait through 3 barks, then I pay the quiet. I rehearse that doorbell on 5 separate evenings so the festival goes stale.</p>
<p>Home is indoors. The yard is a sniff and a return, with a person attached. Parking one outside through a workday wastes the trait that makes them good company, and it leaves a very small dog in weather the coat only partly answers. Mine changes rooms when I change rooms, and he loses the plot if a door shuts him out. The follow is the affection. It is also why a bed lives in the office. Show flesh lands from 3 pounds up to 7, and a dog that light is easy to step on when he shadows my ankles.</p>
<p>Play arrives in bursts. A plush toy gets shaken, paraded, and stuffed under a cushion. I join for a few minutes, then I end it. An overtired spitz gets sharper with the bark. Strangers are announced first and usually accepted if I greet them before he does. Children do better sitting on the floor than chasing. Ears forward and tail curled over the back means he is in the conversation. Ears pinned and tail dropped means I stop the interaction. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pomeranian/">AKC Pomeranian breed page</a> describes this same alert companion, fox face included, and it matches the dog who supervises my sink.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-grooming-guide.html": `<p>This is a double coat, and it mats to the skin if I only skim the top. A slicker and a metal comb are the pair I trust. I part the ruff, the pants, and the plume, and I comb outward from the skin. The fox face gets a lighter pass so the short muzzle hair stays put. Full part-and-comb lands on date 3 and date 5 of my week. The other mornings I hit friction only: behind the ears, under the collar, and the feather on the hind legs.</p>
<p>Seasonally the coat blows. Undercoat lets go in soft clumps that fill a laundry basket, while guard hairs stay and look ragged until new down rises. I rake more often during that blow. Clippers stay in the closet. Shaving off the guard coat removes the layer that shields the skin, and the shed cycle continues underneath the bald look. A bath in the middle of a blow, dried all the way down and then combed, pulls a remarkable amount of dead undercoat. Damp left against the skin smells by morning.</p>
<p>The tail is a plume that tangles at the base. I support the bone and comb the hair downward, then I fluff it back over. Hair between the pads gets trimmed so he can feel the floor. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/pomeranian">VCA Pomeranian grooming and coat notes</a> line up with the comb-to-skin habit I want copied at home. Pretty from the doorway is how mats get a two-week head start.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A toy spitz was kept to live with people and to notice the world, so the week is companionship plus a little mileage. The trachea gets a vote. Hard yanks and endless excitement are how the goose honk starts, so the equipment is a harness and the outing ends while breathing is still easy. I plan 3 short outings on ordinary weekdays. Each one is a sniff around the block or a hallway retrieve, closed while he still has another lap in him. On weekday 5 I add a longer wander on grass, still at his pace.</p>
<p>Indoor work carries the actual job. Kibble goes into a rolled towel. A sit happens before the door opens, which gives the bark a place to land besides a full aria. After any spike of excitement he goes to the bed until the chest is quiet. Summer middays stay inside. The yard is a supervised sniff, then back to the house. An empty yard all afternoon strands an alert dog with every passerby and nobody to report to.</p>
<p>Adults kept for the show ring still sit inside a 3 pound to 7 pound window. A leap off the couch is a bigger event than it looks. I put a ramp by the cushion or I do the lifting myself. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pomeranian/">Pomeranian temperament notes at AKC</a> frame the alert companion I am trying to spend, in the mind more than in the legs. A solved search tires him. A fourth lap of the same block mostly winds the voice.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Tiny babies can crash. Hypoglycemia looks like weakness, a wobble, and a glassy stare when a meal ran late. I set 3 small meals as the floor and I often split the day into 5 feedings while the puppy still fits in a palm. The future bowl is a show weight of 3 to 7 pounds, so the portions look silly and they are still the whole plan. A little soaked kibble stays ready. I watch him after play, because play spends the sugar.</p>
<p>Before any money moves I want patella status on the parents, an eye note that mentions progressive retinal atrophy, and a line about Legg-Calve-Perthes if a relative limped young. I ask what adult coats did in that family. Alopecia X is trunk-hair loss I would rather meet on paper than as a surprise at two years. Collapsing trachea may stay hidden in a baby, so I watch how the parents breathe when the door gets exciting. Dental disease comes later. A finger wipe on the baby teeth still starts now, so the habit exists before tartar does.</p>
<p>The first coat change is part of this year. Puppy fluff lets go and the adult double coat arrives, and that is when tangles start if nobody parts the hair. I comb every evening through the swap. Handling the fox face, the feet, and the plume makes the grooming table boring. Boring is the goal. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/pomeranian">VCA Pomeranian health handout</a> is the overview I read with the breeder's folder spread beside it.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Hair on this dog comes from two layers. Guard hairs draw the fox outline: ruff, pants, and tail. Undercoat sits on the skin, soft and dense, and that is the layer that releases. In a blow I can lift a cloud with a rake while the guard hairs stay planted. The cloud is seasonal. Between blows the house gets a steady sprinkle of both types, on dark sleeves and in the corner where he sleeps.</p>
<p>The comb tells the truth. On calendar days 3 and 5 it reaches the skin and comes back with a visible wad. Surface-only brushing lets the undercoat pack, mat, and then depart in felted pieces that steal live hair on the way out. Friction zones go first: collar line, armpit, back of the thigh. The muzzle sheds less. Those short facial hairs do fall, and they hide until they stick to a damp hand.</p>
<p>Clippers do not stop the follicle from cycling. They hide the evidence until regrowth felts worse than the original coat. I rake, I bathe during a heavy blow, and I dry to the skin before the comb returns. A basket beside the table keeps the blow off my socks. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pomeranian/">double-coat detail on the AKC Pomeranian page</a> is the description I use when someone asks where all this hair was stored. It was on the dog, under the outline, the whole time.</p>`,

  "guides/pomeranian-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The stretch I quote is often 12 to 16 years. That is a long watch on a body kept at 3 to 7 pounds in show flesh, and the length is why small faults become the thing that shrinks the good part. A collapsing trachea can turn doorbell joy into a cough that limits walks for years, and a bad hour can become an airway crisis. Dental disease shrinks comfort. I have seen mouths that should have been cleaned by year 5 wait until the dog stopped chewing.</p>
<p>Luxating patellas shrink the active years when a knee that used to skip starts to stay luxated and the leg gets carried. Legg-Calve-Perthes disease can end sound movement while the dog is still young, which rewrites the middle of that 12 to 16 year stretch. Progressive retinal atrophy shrinks the visual world. Dusk gets hard, then ordinary rooms get hard, even when the legs are willing. Alopecia X seldom ends a life by itself. It does flag a coat that needs a clinic rule-out, so a hormone disease is caught while everyone is still calling the bald trunk cosmetic.</p>
<p>Hypoglycemia is the early threat, aimed at tiny puppies. A baby pulled through a crash still needs the meal rhythm that held the sugar steady. Extra fat on a 3 to 7 pound frame makes the airway and the kneecaps work harder. I want a waist I can see from above, and I recheck the scale when the years get long and the zoomies fade. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/pomeranian">VCA overview of Pomeranian care</a> sits in my folder next to the ages I have actually watched, from the palm-sized baby to the gray muzzle at the far end of 16. I write the age on the folder in ink so the next clinic sees the clock we are actually on.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-pomeranians-get.html": `<p>Show weight is 3 to 7 pounds. That is the band I hold against a finished dog, along with the fox face, the small ears set high, and the tail carried over the back. A newborn does not step out at that weight. I weigh every week. The body stays light, the head looks large for it, and the coat adds visual bulk the scale ignores. I believe the scale.</p>
<p>Most of the ones I have raised are close to their adult pounds by month 8, and the last filling plus the adult coat settles by month 12. After month 12 I stop expecting a big jump and I start expecting the puppy coat to blow. A dog at 5 pounds then can be entirely correct. A dog racing past 7 pounds gets a harder look at calories, because the show line and the knee line are the same small skeleton. I have also kept lovely companions who sat just inside 3 pounds and needed smaller, more frequent meals while they were growing.</p>
<p>Expression finishes in the face without the muzzle going flat. I do not want a baby whose nose is already pushed in. Height stays modest. The impression of size is the coat standing off the body. Bathed and blown out, the same animal looks half as big, and the pounds did not move. I write the weight on a card each month so the fluff cannot talk me into a bigger dinner. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pomeranian/">AKC Pomeranian size and appearance notes</a> are what I show someone who thinks the cloud of hair is the dog. Under the fluff sits that show band plus the fox face, and those two facts finish the measurement.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-health-issues.html": `<p>A red lump at the inner corner of the eye is how cherry eye introduces itself, usually in the juvenile months. The gland that belongs under the third eyelid pops forward and stays angry. Rubbing the face along the rug makes the lump angrier. I book the doctor who handles eyes, and I ask about tucking that gland back, because it helps produce tears. Taking it out and hoping the eye stays comfortable is a plan I want said out loud before any steel is used.</p>
<p>Juvenile cataracts cloud the lens early. A puppy who misses a treat tossed across a dim kitchen, or whose pupil looks smoky, needs an ophthalmologist. I want that exam during the growing year and again if the eye changes. Luxating patellas read as a hop, a hind foot held clear, then a trot that looks ordinary. A kneecap grade belongs on paper. Hemivertebrae turn up in some lines. Wedge-shaped vertebrae, often near the twist of the tail, can leave a dog sore along the back or unsteady behind. Once the knees are cleared and the rear gait still looks neurologic, spinal films are the pictures I request.</p>
<p>The muzzle is short and the skull is brachycephalic, so heat is a stress I plan around. Noisy breathing, a tongue that stays wide, a player who plants all four feet in the sun: that dog comes indoors. Facial wrinkles have to be kept dry. A damp fold grows yeast, and then he scrubs his face on the carpet until skin splits. I dry the fold after water and after meals with a cotton round, and I show the clean cotton so the job has a picture attached to it.</p>
<p>Weight splits into three classes, with a ceiling of 25 pounds. The light class is under 15. A dog at 12 pounds lives in it. Middle is 15 and under 20. The heavy class runs from 20 up to 25. Crossing 25 puts the airway and the knees under a load they were not drawn for. Years I quote for this breed often sit from 11 through 13. Inside that stretch I book knees and eyes by year 3, and I look at the eyes again around year 5 when the first certificate was clean. Tuxedo markings, black laid over white, change where dirt shows. They do not change which tests I ask for.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC patella guidance for small breeds</a> is the orthopedic frame I open when the hop shows up. I keep the eye note in the same folder, because a clear knee and a cloudy lens are two different afternoons at the clinic.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-temperament.html": `<p>Play arrives already switched on. The Boston who shared my sofa carried a toy into every room and started a wrestling match with the rug. These are indoor companions. Black-and-white markings make the game easy to follow and the mud easy to spot on the chest. A short muzzle puts a heat ceiling on the fun. I shut the game down when breathing turns loud, and I move it onto the cool floor. Pushing the same dog to continue in a warm room makes him frantic and then flat. That choice is behavior work sitting beside airway shape.</p>
<p>With their own people they clown. With a newcomer they are often polite once the newcomer sits. A face coming down from standing height, aimed at a short nose, is a lot of pressure. Guests sit. He climbs up if he chooses to. Children who play gently on the floor do well. Children who chase do poorly, because he chases back and that muzzle is a bad tool for a long grabbing contest.</p>
<p>Build changes the bounce. A 12 pound adult in the group under 15 is a lap acrobat. A dog still capped at 25 pounds, in the heavy slot that begins at 20 pounds and stops at 25, hits harder when he launches off a cushion. The middle group, 15 and under 20, is where several of mine landed. A noon jog in July is a poor idea for every one of those bodies. The clock I keep for them often runs from 11 years out to 13. The playful brain outlasts the joints that would prefer a softer game. I keep 3 toys in a basket and I trade them for 5 minutes of tug followed by a long settle on a mat, so the clown gets a finish and then a rest.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boston-terrier/">AKC Boston Terrier breed page</a> names the companion I have been picking toys up after. I believe the page about the character. I believe the room temperature about when the game is over.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-grooming-guide.html": `<p>The jacket is short. The wrinkles are the chore that matters. Cotton rounds live by the sink for the fold over the nose and the folds beside the mouth. After a drink, after breakfast, and after any roll outdoors, I dry those folds until the cotton comes away clean. A wet crease on a brachycephalic face is how the itch gets started. The short muzzle parks that crease close to the eyes and the teeth, so the hand stays light and I quit the moment he pulls away.</p>
<p>Black-and-white hair sheds short strands and picks up skin oil. A hound glove, or a cloth that is barely damp, handles the shine on the black and the mud on the white. Baths stay uncommon. Mild shampoo goes on the body. The face gets a wet cloth more often than a spray, because water standing in the nose fold undoes the drying. Wednesday carries pass 3 for the folds. Friday carries pass 5. Those two named passes saved more skin than any elaborate bath rhythm I copied off a chart.</p>
<p>Nails stay short so the little jumps he loves land square. Ears stand open. I look, I smell, and I wipe only the surface I can see. Eyes get a damp cotton sweep from the inner corner outward, especially when staining tracks down into the face fold. Teeth crowd behind the short muzzle. A small canine toothbrush across the canines feels clumsy and earns its place. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/boston-terrier">VCA Boston Terrier care handout</a> is the sheet I hand people who assume a short coat means pockets stay full and the dog stays untouched.</p>
<p>I also open the white chest after a walk. Grass stain hides in that bib, and a stained bib left damp overnight spreads trouble toward the folds. A quiet rinse at the sink beats a week of face-rubbing on the sofa. The used cotton goes in the trash where I can see the job happened.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-exercise-guide.html": `<p>House company and play are what this dog was kept for, so the week is play with a thermostat on the wall. Ordinary days get 3 blocks: morning tug on a rug, an evening sniff while the air is cool, and a living-room recall that asks for a sit plus a down. The longer outing lands on day 5, still in the cool part of the clock, on grass when the park allows it. A short muzzle and a brachycephalic airway write the stop. Loud breath, a tongue that will not slim down, or feet that plant means the outing is finished and we head inside.</p>
<p>Heat becomes rest even when I had written something else on the calendar. Summer middays turn into indoor scent games. A treat hides under a cup. He wins, he sleeps, and I refuse the extra round he asks for with a wag. Winter lets the walk stretch. Rain is tolerable on this coat if the face folds are dried at the door before the couch.</p>
<p>I trim repeated high impact. Launching off furniture is his invention. A ramp shows up once the play has already been plenty. A harness spreads a pull more evenly than a collar on a dog whose airway is already abbreviated. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boston-terrier/">Boston Terrier profile at AKC</a> matches the playful indoor dog I am trying to tire without overheating. A spent brain and a cool chest count as success. A panting clown at midday counts as a miss.</p>
<p>Evenings that end with a chew on the mat do more for the next morning than one more lap. I watch the noise of the breath the way other people watch a watch. When the sound changes, the game changes. That single habit has saved more July afternoons than any mileage goal I used to chase.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The parents stand on a scale before a puppy lands in my lap. Three classes exist: under 15 pounds, then 15 and under 20, then 20 up to 25. Passing 25 is past the line I accept. A mother weighing 12 pounds sits in the light class. I ask which class each parent finished, because the frame follows that record more honestly than a sales adjective. The years ahead often cover a run from 11 up to 13, so the folder is a wager on a long middle, and I treat the paperwork with that horizon in mind.</p>
<p>Juvenile cataracts can show while the body is still stacking. I want an eye exam on the parents and a plan to examine the puppy before the first birthday. A smoky lens is a finding I act on. Cherry eye can pop in a youngster as well. I recognize the red gland at the inner corner, and the afternoon becomes a clinic trip, with the eye kept off the carpet. Patella results on the parents matter before cash moves. Hemivertebrae in some lines mean I ask whether a spine was ever filmed on a relative who moved oddly in the rear.</p>
<p>Meals through this growing year stay measured so we land inside a class and remain at or beneath that 25 pound cap. I reweigh every couple of weeks and I park the number where I will actually see it. Drying the wrinkles starts the day a face fold can hold water. Heat rules start the same week. Play, then rest. Midday yard time in summer stays off the list. Muzzle, feet, and tail get a gentle handle each evening so a real exam feels familiar.</p>
<p>Handling should feel boring by month 3. By month 5 I want one clinic visit that watches gait, eyes, and knees together. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/boston-terrier">VCA page on Boston Terrier health</a> is what I read before the check is written. A marked-up puppy face is cute enough to rush a deposit. The cute can sit one evening while the eye note gets read twice.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Short coat means short hairs. They exit follicles across the torso, the thigh, and the shoulder, and they are stiff enough to weave into cloth. Black hairs disappear on a dark couch and shout on a white shirt. White hairs from the markings do the reverse. Navy pants collect them every time I stand up. Volume stays lower than what a double coat dumps. Presence is the issue. A few hairs are simply always on the move.</p>
<p>Cheeks shed a little, and those hairs stick inside damp wrinkles. Drying a fold often lifts white strands along with the moisture. That is cast hair held by wet skin. Leave it and the crease gets angry. The seasonal rise is mild. Spring puts a little more in the glove. I never see a basket of blown undercoat. Real clumps send me hunting a skin problem. The calendar is the second guess, after the skin.</p>
<p>An adult at 12 pounds, placed in the class under 15, gives up less absolute hair than a dog in the heavier class that runs 20 pounds through 25, because the surface area is smaller. Every class still stops at 25 pounds. A span quoted around 11 years and again around 13 means this low scatter is a long fact of the house. It outlasts the puppy coat. I glove once the week count hits 3, and I glove again at 5. Mud on the white chest gets wiped, because grit clings to hairs that are ready to let go. A soft rubber curry rolls hair into the tool. A harsh slicker made for furnished breeds scratches this skin. I stop when the skin pinks.</p>
<p>Bath water frees a ring of hair in the tub. Gloving first keeps the drain happier. Wrinkles get dried afterward so loose hairs stay out of a wet fold. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boston-terrier/">coat and color notes for the Boston Terrier</a> describe the short hairs I keep lifting off my cuffs. Yesterday that strand was growing on a rib. No second layer was hiding a winter dump underneath it.</p>
<p>I shake the bed blanket outside twice a week and I look at what falls. The amount is modest and the color split is obvious, black on one shake and white on the next if I have been sitting in different shirts. That visual is how I explain the shed to someone who expected a coated breed's blizzard and then blamed the vacuum for a job it cannot finish alone.</p>`,

  "guides/boston-terrier-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Often the years run 11 to 13. I have stood at both ends of that line, and the shorter stories usually had breathing, eyes, or the spine in the lead. Heat stress in a short-muzzled brachycephalic dog can cut a life inside one afternoon. That is the sudden version. The slow version is a summer without enough cool rest, the dog inflamed and worn out on a loop. Wrinkles left wet shrink comfort. Infected facial skin can keep him pacing at night for years.</p>
<p>Juvenile cataracts can take sight early and change how safely he moves through whatever remains. Cherry eye, when the gland is damaged and tears run short, can feed a dry eye that bothers him in every bright room. Luxating patellas shrink walks once a knee locks. Hemivertebrae in some lines shrink the easy years if the cord is squeezed and the rear weakens. Weight past the class caps turns the volume up on all of it. Classes step from under 15, through 15 and under 20, up to the group from 20 to 25, and 25 is the line I refuse to cross.</p>
<p>Year 3 is the reminder for eyes, knees, and a breathing note already in the chart. Year 5 is the reminder for teeth and for a weight that still matches the class he grew into. A playful indoor mind hides pain well, so a quieter toy session is a signal I trust ahead of the cake. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/boston-terrier">VCA Hospitals page for Boston Terriers</a> is the overview paired with that calendar. Dogs who reach the far side usually had someone who treated noon heat as a serious appointment.</p>
<p>Hands on the ribs every Sunday keep the scoop honest. A thickening waist hides badly on a tuxedo chest if I actually look, and it hides perfectly in a photo. He will still ask for tug when a knee would prefer to skip the landing. I answer with a floor game and a ramp. I count that answer as part of the year count, right beside any lab slip.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-boston-terriers-get.html": `<p>Adults fall into three weight classes, and the ceiling is firm at 25 pounds. Under 15 is light. A dog who weighs 12 pounds belongs in that light group. Middle covers 15 and under 20. Top covers 20 up to 25. I say the ceiling aloud because puppy fat gets mistaken for a promotion in class. A light-framed puppy pushed by extra calories can cross 25 and remain there, and then every landing costs more than the bones were built to pay.</p>
<p>Filling follows a small-dog clock. Height and bone I have tracked usually settle between month 8 and month 12. Once month 12 arrives I know which class we occupy. Chest breadth, the short muzzle, and the black-and-white pattern are obvious long before the pounds lock. Males and females overlap more here than in a giant breed. I ask each parent's finished weight in pounds, class named beside it. A guess with no scale on it gets set aside.</p>
<p>Month 3 shows a fraction of the adult, feet small and square. At month 5 a lean toward the light class or the top class usually appears, though a late spurt has fooled me more than once. Home weights go on a card. I recheck that card after vacation weeks, when extra bites test the class ceiling. People read a broad, wrinkled puppy as older and heavier than the number in my hand. The short face creates the illusion. Breathing and heat cap the work I ask while the body fills. Floor play, a cool walk, then a nap.</p>
<p>The adult I am feeding toward can breathe, can step down without a stumble, and can stand inside one class without a debate at the scale. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boston-terrier/">AKC description of Boston Terrier weight classes</a> is the note I print for families who only heard the word small. Small here still ends at a number, and that number is 25. I recheck after the first heat wave, because a summer of short walks and the same scoop is how a correct puppy becomes an over-class adult.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-health-issues.html": `<p>A drug list is taped inside the cabinet because of MDR1. Dogs of this breed who carry the mutation can react badly to certain common medicines, so the result has to sit in the chart before a new hospital reaches for a dose. I speak the name MDR1 at every check-in. Collie eye anomaly, progressive retinal atrophy, and cataracts are the eye trio I schedule around. CEA is developmental and often found while the puppy is young. PRA takes sight over time, dim rooms first. Cataracts cloud the lens. An ophthalmologist's certificate is the paper. One clear look at month 3 leaves the grown eye unknown. I repeat eyes around year 5.</p>
<p>Hip dysplasia shows as a slow push up, a shortened stride behind, or a bunny hop. Hip radiographs are the pictures. Merle is permitted. Stacking merle on merle can yield puppies who are deaf and blind. I treat that match as a failure of welfare. The patches are beside the point. Merle with a solid partner is the match I will discuss, and hearing plus eyes still get checked, because the rest of the disease list keeps existing on a patterned coat.</p>
<p>These dogs are smaller than an Australian Shepherd, and they still work through pain the way herding dogs do. A cheerful walk does not reassure me when the sit is slow. Male withers sit between 14 and 18 inches, and female withers sit between 13 and 17. Enough bone for hips to matter, and small enough that a sore dog will still try to steer the children. Years often fall near 12 and 13. I want that span with working eyes and hips that can lie down without a groan. A second copy of the drug caution rides inside the travel crate, because a vacation clinic never sees the kitchen cabinet.</p>
<p>I also watch a merle litter for a puppy who sleeps through a dropped pan. Deafness from the doubled pattern shows up as a startle that never comes. I would rather learn it in the breeder's kitchen than after money has moved. The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC eye and hip clearances for herding dogs</a> is the checklist I hold against the folder. Missing eye paper or missing hip film ends the visit. Temperament talk can wait until the folder is real.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-temperament.html": `<p>The broom shifted and the dog dropped into a crouch. One kitchen, whole character. This is a herding dog built on a shorter leg than the Aussie I worked years ago, and the smaller outline did not retire the job. AKC recognized the breed in 2015. Dogs I knew before that year were already moving stock and gathering the household under older registrations. They still need a task. With no task, the task becomes the cat, the child, the vacuum, or my ankles.</p>
<p>Work gets handed out on purpose. A herding ball inside a fence for 20 minutes, then a mat settle, then a chance to move me from the gate to the shed. A trained adult may go 40 minutes on a big day, and I stop while the halts are still clean. The span I expect often sits around 12 years or 13, which is a lot of nights of pretend sheep if I supply none. Merle is allowed. Work looks alike on spots and on a solid color. The release matters more to me than the patches.</p>
<p>Bright, and vocal when their people do something new. A window bark is herding aimed at empty air. I answer with an assignment: find the toy, take the bed, walk the fence with me. Lessons stick when they are fair and when they end. Weekdays hold 3 brief lessons. A slightly harder one lands when the week reaches 5. Then the dog is off duty. An off-duty herder banned from watching everything gets frustrated. I choose the thing he is allowed to watch.</p>
<p>They attach hard, and a rough stranger can earn a long look. Social minutes should succeed, with a door the dog may use to leave. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-american-shepherd/">breed profile for this smaller herding dog</a> describes this herding mind on a reduced frame. The crouch at the broom still surprises guests who expected a lap ornament, and I let them watch one full minute before I explain the job. The page sets the expectation. The broom tells me whether the crouch is still in this particular dog.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-grooming-guide.html": `<p>A moderate double coat shows best on the pants, across the chest, and in the ruff behind the ears. Undercoat yields to a rake. Feathering yields to a slicker used with a soft wrist. I part behind the ears and through the armpit, because mats begin quietly on a dog who looks tidy from the door. Merle and solid take the same tools. Pigment is only pigment.</p>
<p>The full pass happens when the week hits 3, and it happens again when the week hits 5. Rake, comb feathers, check ears, trim the hair between pads. Remaining days are a hand-through on the floor, pausing only if fingers snag. Baths follow mud or a seasonal cast. The dryer stays warm and moving until the undercoat is actually dry. Damp coat on skin becomes an itch overnight. Belly fur stays clear enough to see skin, since grass awns collect there on a dog who works low.</p>
<p>Tail feathers collect burs. I comb from the tip toward the body while the other hand supports the base, pulling hair without yanking the tail itself. Feet get trimmed in weeks when the torso can wait. Nails meet a grinder after the bath, foot already clean. Less coat than the Aussie jacket I learned on, and it still punishes a skim. The handout I trust is <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-american-shepherd">coat-care guidance from the VCA shepherd sheet</a>. Pretty pants with a mat buried underneath are a failure I can feel before I see it.</p>
<p>Tall grass days end with a comb through the feathers before any dog touches the bed. Seeds travel. Burr chaff travels with them. The collar line gets a separate pass because friction felts it faster than the middle of the back. The comb hangs with the leash so the check happens at the door, while the mud is still honest. I wipe the comb on a white towel afterward so the undercoat I just pulled is visible.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Herding is the original work, so the week ought to move something. A ball counts when stock is unavailable. A flock is better on the weeks I can borrow one. Rest is scheduled beside the work, because this dog will chase until pads bleed and will not budget hips on his own. Monday is a 20 minute block with a herding ball in a fenced yard, plus a down that truly stops the chase. Tuesday is a long sniff and brain work: stays at a distance, a recall away from the moving toy, a settle while a different dog goes by. Wednesday is rest, mat and chew, with no secret rehearsal after I look away.</p>
<p>Thursday shortens the ball work. Friday moves indoors for nose games. Saturday is the long block, up to 40 minutes of stock or ball when the dog is grown, fit, and still thinking. Sunday stays empty of drills. A span that often reads 12 years through 13 is too valuable to spend lame, so puppy months and early adult months stay lighter than that Saturday. I scatter 3 easy neighborhood walks through the week for decompression. Each work block ends with 5 minutes of settle before the door, while he is still tight. Heat cuts Saturday. Cold is usually welcome. Hot pavement and jump repeats build a limp he will hide because the job still feels good.</p>
<p>Shorter legs than a full-size Aussie do not make a glance at the yard into a complete day. Distance scales down. The mind stays employed. Boredom shows up as bedtime herding of the household. Spending those 20 minutes on purpose beats bargaining over ankle nips after dark. The page I show people who believed a reduced height cancelled instinct is the <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-american-shepherd/">working history behind this smaller shepherd</a>. The height changed. The week still has to prove the instinct has a legal outlet, or he will invent a worse one.</p>
<p>I log the stops along with the minutes. A session full of clean downs is a success even if it ends early. A session of nonstop chase that I have to body-block is a failure even if the clock looks impressive. That log is how I keep a willing dog sound into the second half of the span.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The folder comes out before any transfer. I want MDR1 results, an eye sheet listing cataracts, collie eye anomaly, and progressive retinal atrophy, plus hip information on both parents. Merle is allowed. A merle-to-merle mating risks deafness together with blindness, so color genetics of each parent get asked before I admire spots. Merle beside a solid coat is the pairing I will talk about, and a hearing check still happens if anyone sounds unsure.</p>
<p>AKC recognition came in 2015. Older dogs in a family may carry history under a former registry name. I ask what the grandparents' papers actually were. The puppy circling my shoes is still a herding dog, a step down in height from an Aussie. This first year is when circling learns a stop. Engagement starts at 3 minutes and grows slowly. Later I add a settle of 5 minutes after play so the task has an edge where it ends.</p>
<p>The card on the fridge lists a male from 14 inches through 18, and a female from 13 inches through 17. Shoulder measurements get a date beside them. Photos at week 20, then another set at week 40, because coat and memory both lie. The years ahead often read 12 and 13, and food, flooring, and allowed jumps are how those years open. Repeated impact stays off the menu while bone is growing. Instinct gets a slow ball on grass and a lot of halts. With the papers spread beside the bowl I read <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-american-shepherd">the VCA puppy health sheet for this herder</a>. I also photograph the side once a month so shoulder height has a witness besides memory.</p>
<p>A stand for a pretend exam, nail touches, and an ear lift happen before handling becomes optional in his mind. Light puppies are charming. Charm is a weak reason to skip the MDR1 line. I want that result the same day I learn the grandparents' names. Social minutes belong in the year too. One quiet place, time to watch, departure while he is still soft. Stairs in a stack can wait. The couch is a lift until growth plates have had their time. Ribs take a light press under a coat that fibs about the waist.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Moderate double coat is the source. Undercoat packs the neck, the chest, the pants, and the thigh, and that layer lifts in handfuls when the season turns. Guard hairs build the outline and depart more quietly, a few stuck to the car seat at a time. Leg and tail feathering shed at the tips and also mat, so the wad in my hand is sometimes cast hair trapped among live hairs. A floor sweep misses the origin. Origin is skin, especially under a collar and where he curls into the sofa arm.</p>
<p>During a blow the rake fills fast and the outline looks moth-eaten until fresh undercoat returns. Between blows the baseboard still collects a tumble every couple of days. Withers from 14 inches to 18 on males, and from 13 inches to 17 on females, still leave less jacket than the Aussies I have stripped out, and the rooms notice anyway. A heavy blow has taken me 20 minutes of honest raking. Neglected pants that had felted have taken closer to 40. Living with a span of 12 years or 13 means that rake stays on the calendar for a long time. Puppyhood ending does not end it.</p>
<p>I refuse to skip the comb when the week marker says 3, and I refuse again when it says 5, outside of blow season. Inside a blow I rake daily and bag the wool before it hitchhikes through the kitchen. Bathing a dirty blowing coat with no rake first sends felt down the drain. The order is rake, bathe, dry all the way, rake once more. Pale hairs and merle hairs show on dark cloth. Dark red and black hairs show on sheets. Color edits the evidence. Follicles keep the same schedule.</p>
<p>Ear feathering is where cast undercoat pretends to be a simple tangle. A comb that reaches skin tells me which I am holding. A comb full of hair on a dog who was "brushed" yesterday means the brush never met the shedding layer. When someone calls the breed low-shed because the dog is the smaller herder, I read aloud the <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-american-shepherd/">registry coat paragraph for this herding breed</a>. Volume changes with size. The double coat remains. I keep a pale sheet on the crate so the week's output is visible, and that sheet corrects my optimism every spring. Those spring sheet checks add time and they stop me from discovering the blow only after the couch has already gone gray. The bag of loose coat leaves the house the same day.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-american-shepherd-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The count I give families is 12 to 13 years. I have celebrated each of those birthdays, and I have watched the front of the span get eaten when eyes or hips failed early. Progressive retinal atrophy shrinks what he can see. The house stays workable and the dark yard comes apart. Cataracts can claim the lens on a separate clock. Collie eye anomaly is there from early life and can mean sight was never ordinary, which rewrites the whole stretch. MDR1 does not trim a life by existing on a lab report. It trims a life when the wrong drug goes in. That cut can be refused, which is why the paper stays where a visiting clinic will read it.</p>
<p>Hip dysplasia trims the moving years. Hurt herding dogs keep offering to work, so the limp shows up late. Merle stacked on merle can leave deaf and blind puppies. A life missing hearing and sight is a different shortness, even while a heartbeat continues. I will not make that match. Cancer and ordinary organ failure still arrive inside 12 to 13 years. Those I cannot put on a calendar. Body condition, a job that skips pounding the joints every single day, and eye exams after the baby visit are the pieces I can put on one.</p>
<p>Year 3 gets a fresh eye look and a hip talk if the gait shifted. Year 5 gets the eyes again and a weight check scaled to a male shoulder of 14 inches through 18, or a female shoulder of 13 inches through 17. Fluffy dogs get overfed. Their minds get underused. Both mistakes chew the ends of the count. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-american-shepherd">VCA Hospitals Miniature American Shepherd handout</a> is where I send someone who wants diseases listed next to years. Reaching 13 with usable sight and a dog who still lies down smoothly is the purchase the folder was meant to make.</p>
<p>I also keep the drug list updated when a new clinic joins the care. A perfect hip film does nothing if a sensitive dog gets the wrong injection on a travel day. The fridge list is lifespan equipment, as concrete as the eye certificate.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-miniature-american-shepherds-get.html": `<p>Males stand 14 to 18 inches. Females stand 13 to 17. The tape goes at the withers, the dog square, neck fluff ignored. A reduced herding build is the idea, and the registry added this breed in 2015. Inches are what the registry publishes. Pounds wander. A slight female has read 20 on my clinic scale. A substantial male has read 40. Both sat inside the inch lines and both looked like the breed. The scale is not how I choose a puppy.</p>
<p>Development runs longer than a toy's and shorter than a giant's. Height I have followed usually declares itself between month 10 and month 16. Month 10 shows serious legs and a chest that is still narrow. Month 16 has most of the height set, with the body catching up. Side photos at week 20 and again around week 40 reveal the chest drop. Looking every few days lies. Coat is costume. Fresh off a blow, the dog looks reduced and the withers number is unchanged.</p>
<p>Males drift toward the top of 14 to 18 and females toward 13 to 17, with overlap that has embarrassed my guesses. I ask for parent heights that were measured. Memory is a sloppy tape. Merle puppies and solid puppies share one growth curve. Month 3 is only a baseline on the card. Month 5 starts to suggest the lower band or the upper. Work stays honest for the age while that happens. A herding puppy will outrun what growing joints appreciate. The adult I want can perform the job inside those inches, wearing a moderate double coat over sound hips.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-american-shepherd/">height lines on the AKC Miniature American Shepherd page</a> sit beside the wicket. A listing that shouts weight still gets the wicket. Readings of 20 and of 40 can both be honest and still be the wrong headline. Inches go on the folder with a pen. Pounds stay a footnote about food. I also note whether he was standing square or leaning, because a lean steals an inch and then the whole growth chart drifts. Next month starts from that same stance, written beside the number.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-health-issues.html": `<p>The skip I watch is a luxating patella. A hind leg lifts, a small hop follows, then the trot pretends nothing occurred. I want the kneecap scored on a form. Hips belong on the list as well, which startles people who pictured only a lap. Stiffness, a sway, a refusal of a hop he used to make. Films of the hips answer that one. Cataracts cloud the lens. A gray cast in the pupil sends me to book an eye exam. Waiting on the cloud to declare itself "only age" has never paid off, including when the dog is a young adult.</p>
<p>Chondrodysplasia changes cartilage and bone growth. Legs that look far shorter and more crooked than the parents' legs are a reason to ask if that diagnosis is in the line. Some screening lists include a liver-shunt check. I use that name before the face wins the visit. Drop ears need inspection after play. I fold the leather, look down the canal, and dry it when grass was wet. Head shaking through the evening means I skipped that look on a Havanese who otherwise ate dinner like everything was fine.</p>
<p>This muzzle runs longer than the one I see on a Shih Tzu, so the eye and the bite I inspect sit differently. Joint risk and eye risk stay on the page anyway. Patella scoring and an eye exam get booked close to year 3, and the ophthalmologist comes back at year 5. A span that often reaches 14 to 16 makes those middle checks worth the drive. The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC patella and hip program details</a> name the orthopedic papers I want before I write "sound" in my own notes.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-temperament.html": `<p>Company is physical with this Cuban toy. Mine changed rooms whenever I did, bathroom included. Social means a hello, a request to be lifted, a need to stand inside the conversation. An empty day frays that dog. I arrange hours so people are around, and I teach a settle so those people can finish a call.</p>
<p>Barking is part of the package. It greets, it demands, and it alarms, sometimes inside a single minute. Quiet gets paid after 3 barks. The doorbell gets rehearsed across 5 evenings until the show goes stale. The muzzle sticks out past the face I am used to on a Shih Tzu. Expression reads softer, and he still studies people the way a companion does. Play needs a partner. The toy returns when I hold the far end.</p>
<p>A stranger usually earns a cheerful hello after a check-in with me. He approaches on his own feet. Pushing his body at guests is off my list. Children who sit and allow a climb into a lap get the best version. Constant handling of the silky coat helps manners when puppy hands were ordinary from the start. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/havanese/">AKC Havanese breed page</a> describes the social toy I keep making space for. The page is right about the company. The settle is what keeps the bark from becoming the entire dog.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Skip seven days and this silky coat mats. I learned it on a Saturday, hair behind one ear felted into a plug, skin tender under my fingers. Growth is long and fine. There is no seasonal undercoat I can rake once and forget. Tangles form where hair rubs: behind ears, in the armpit, under the collar, through the skirt on the hind legs. A metal comb begins at the skin. I label two full comb days, 3 and 5, and I keep both. Leftover days still cover ears and the rub spots.</p>
<p>Baths wait for dirt or for a product that helps a comb-out. Wetting a felted coat and hoping is a mess I have already made once. Mats come apart dry, fingers pinning hair at the root so skin stays still. Scissors touch a mat only when skin is at risk. Face hair gets a wipe each day. Because this muzzle projects farther than a Shih Tzu muzzle, the beard holds water along extra length. I dry the beard after the water bowl.</p>
<p>Drop ears conceal moisture. After bathing and after yard play I dry the canal entrance and comb the ear fringe. Pad hair gets trimmed so the foot can grip. A topknot or two small bands keep eyes clear when the coat stays long. A shorter pet trim is a fair family choice. The comb remains. The territory shrinks. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/havanese">VCA Havanese grooming notes</a> match the rhythm I abandoned the week that plug formed. Rhythm means the comb shows up on its days. A rescue session after the skin is already sore is how I know the rhythm failed.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A social companion is the old job, so the week is shared time plus enough movement to sleep. Adults I kept weighed 7 to 13 pounds and often lived a stretch of 14 years through 16. That is a long interest to maintain in a little body. Weekdays hold 3 walks or play blocks, social and sniffy. At point 5 the plan stretches into a friend's kitchen, or trick work in a room he has not mapped. Being with someone matters as much as the step total.</p>
<p>Tug, a soft retrieve, and tricks spend the brain. I quit before barking turns frantic. Overtired, this breed gets louder, and louder spends the evening I wanted. Rest is written in. One afternoon is a lap plus a chew. Heat gets respect. A small dog under long silky hair can overheat. Outings go early. Water comes along. Drop ears are dried after damp grass.</p>
<p>Leaping off furniture is the workout they design themselves. A ramp and a floor game beat letting that 7 to 13 pound body fly off the couch back night after night across a 14 to 16 year life. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/havanese/">Havanese companion profile at AKC</a> fits the week I keep. A finished trick and a cool walk beat a zoom that ends in an argument with the hallway mirror.</p>
<p>Ugly weather still gets a brain outing. Find the keys. Sit before the door. Spin for one kibble. The body stays light while a person pays attention, which is the work. Swapping people out and stacking mileage is how this companion turns loud and odd. I have watched that swap fail in apartments that looked fine on paper.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-puppy-guide.html": `<p>A comb comes home with the puppy. Silky strands mat when a week is missed, and baby coat tangles faster while adult hair rises underneath. Ears, armpits, and skirt get combed every evening. If the habit is theater by month 3, plugs are already being cut. By month 5 a full comb-out should happen without a battle, because feet and muzzle were touched from night one.</p>
<p>Cash waits on patella notes, hip notes, an eye report mentioning cataracts, a remark on chondrodysplasia, and the liver-shunt check if that breeder's list includes one. Some lists print the words. Some households have never met the phrase. I ask regardless. Drop ears are inspected after grass, including on the visit before I commit. I want to watch somebody fold the leather and actually look.</p>
<p>Size ahead of us is roughly 8.5 inches up to 11.5, with weight from 7 pounds to 13, and a muzzle that projects beyond a Shih Tzu muzzle. Year one is steady growth, meeting calm people, and giving the bark a job before it hardens. A Cuban toy companion raised on chaos becomes an ornament that shouts. Outings happen. People get to be boring. We leave while he is still sweet. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/havanese">VCA Havanese health handout</a> is the sheet beside the folder. Charm does not sign the check. The papers do.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-shedding-guide.html": `<p>What comes off is silky hair that grows and sheds in strands, closer to a brush full of long hair than to a spitz dumping undercoat. Every session shows them: fine lengths from ears, skirt, tail, and face furnishings. Some were ready to drop. Some snapped inside a tangle. Skip combing and the cast hair remains. It felts into living coat and sits on skin as a mat. A missed week is how a shave or a sore patch gets scheduled.</p>
<p>Seasonal increase stays mild and stays the same hair type. I do not pull a basket of undercoat the way a Pomeranian blow will. More strands show up in the comb, and more fluff shows under the bed. Bodies of 7 to 13 pounds throw a modest volume. A run of years often quoted from 14 through 16 turns that modest daily bit into a long friendship with a lint roller. Pale coats display dirt. Dark coats display shed on a light sofa. Visibility changes. Follicles do not take the season off.</p>
<p>Marker 3 and marker 5 are when the comb goes fully to the skin and I study the yield. Hair after a real session can be ordinary casting. Mats in the comb mean I fell behind. The beard sheds and stains. Face hair gets washed so loose bits stay out of old food. Fringe on the drop ear pins cast hair to the leather. I comb the fringe and check the canal, since trapped hair plus damp is how an ear complaint starts. On a muzzle that projects past a Shih Tzu beard line, the ends shed and need a trim to clear the water bowl.</p>
<p>A pale towel on the couch arm, shaken outside, tells the truth faster than staring at the dog. Silky hair hides inside the coat while it is already all over the room. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/havanese/">silky-coat notes on the AKC Havanese page</a> describe what I am combing. If the notes and the towel argue, the towel wins. I date the towel shakes in the margin of the feeding card so a "sudden" shed has a week of evidence behind it. A second comb in the car catches skirt tangles after a damp walk before they dry into the next day's mat. That comb hangs on its own hook with this dog's kit.</p>`,

  "guides/havanese-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The span I give is 14 to 16 years. Silver has shown up in the silky coat of a dog who still insisted on following me down the hall. Cuts to that span usually come from joints, eyes, or a liver problem that was printed on a screening list and then skipped. Luxating patellas shrink later years when each outdoor trip hurts a knee. Hip disease does similar work from the pelvis. A dog who will not climb to the cushion anymore is reporting pain honestly, even while he still wants the room. Cataracts can remove vision mid-span and turn a social dog into one who startles.</p>
<p>Severe chondrodysplasia changes growth early and can mean orthopedic pain across the rest of those 14 to 16 years. A liver shunt left unaddressed can shorten the story from the puppy months, well before any gray muzzle. Certain breeder sheets name a liver-shunt screen for that reason, and I want the result prior to a deposit. Drop ears do not decide the lifespan. A chronic ear that nobody inspects after play can sour the good years until eating and sleep both slide.</p>
<p>At year 3 I book knees, hips, and eyes together. When year 5 arrives the eye doctor returns, along with a weight check against 7 to 13 pounds. Extra fat across a long life grinds patellas down early. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/havanese">Havanese care overview published by VCA</a> stays with those year marks. Hall-following at 15 is the outcome the folder was supposed to purchase, and it depends on the boring checks more than on the coat people admire.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-havanese-get.html": `<p>Grown dogs stand 8.5 to 11.5 inches and weigh 7 to 13 pounds. Both figures matter, because silk lies. Full length makes 9 pounds resemble a heavier animal. Combed out and trimmed a little, the same animal looks fragile. Scale plus wicket close the debate. The head avoids a flat toy look. This muzzle projects past the one I expect on a Shih Tzu, even on a small body.</p>
<p>Growth follows a toy clock. I measure again at month 8 and at month 12. Month 8 usually places height inside the 8.5 to 11.5 window, with weight moving toward the 7 to 13 pound band. Month 12 should find bone nearly done and coat still changing. Month 3 is a handful of silk, feet neat and square. When month 5 arrives I guess low end versus high end, and the guess is often wrong, which is why the scale stays in the routine. I reweigh after any trim, since a long coat can hide a couple of pounds of silk, and the wicket visit happens on the same floor each time so the numbers compare. Parent cards stay in the same sleeve as the puppy card.</p>
<p>Males and females share that inch span. Parent measurements and parent weights at maturity go on the card, then the puppy's own numbers take over. Early racing toward 13 pounds means fewer treats. Sitting under 7 pounds means a harder look at appetite and at whether a liver-shunt check ever happened. Failing to grow is medical. A correct adult who weighs 7 pounds is simply small and correct. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/havanese/">AKC size lines for the Havanese</a> go on the fridge, inches first and pounds second, coat left out of the math.</p>
<p>Side photos on the same rug keep the legs honest when silk tries to costume them. I compare month 8 with month 12 before I change the food amount. Costume is allowed to be pretty. The scoop still comes from the scale.</p>`,
};
